# Eagle-required badges (part 1) — checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.citizenship-in-the-community
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/skills/merit-badges/digital-resource-guides/citizenship-community/contents/ | Citizenship in the Community Digital Resource Guide (scouting.org)
@desc Citizenship in the Community looks at how local government, services and volunteers shape the place you live, and asks you to take part: attending a public meeting, interviewing someone in local government, volunteering, and presenting what you learned about your community.
1 d What community citizenship means :: What good citizenship in your community means, its rights, duties and obligations, and how you can show it.
2 a Mapping your community :: One of two options for exploring your community’s services, landmarks and resources.
2a a Option A :: Mapping community services and landmarks and their distances from your home.
2b a Option B :: The alternative exploration option described in the official requirement.
3 a A public meeting :: Attending an approved local government or court meeting and analyzing an issue discussed there.
3a a Attend the meeting :: Attending an approved in-person local government or court meeting.
3b d An issue with different views :: Explaining an issue from the meeting where opinions differed and which view you favor.
4 a Investigating a community issue :: Choosing a local issue, learning which part of local government handles it, and interviewing someone involved.
4a k Who handles it :: Identifying which branch or office of local government deals with the issue.
4b a Interview :: Interviewing someone from that office, with approval.
4c d Sharing what you learned :: Sharing what you learned with your counselor.
5 d A film about community service :: Watching an approved film about people who improved their community and discussing its lessons.
6 k Taxpayer-funded services :: Community services paid for by taxes and why they matter.
7 s Charity and volunteering :: Researching charitable organizations, choosing one, and volunteering with it for the required time.
7a k Three organizations :: Identifying charitable organizations outside Scouting that serve your community.
7b k Researching one :: Researching one organization’s work.
7c s Volunteering :: Learning how young people can help and volunteering with the organization, then discussing the experience.
8 a Community presentation :: Preparing and giving a presentation about your community’s history, culture, strengths and challenges.

@owner mb.citizenship-in-the-nation
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/skills/merit-badges/digital-resource-guides/citizenship-nation/contents/ | Citizenship in the Nation Digital Resource Guide (scouting.org)
@desc Citizenship in the Nation covers how the United States is governed — the Constitution, the three branches, founding documents and national issues — and connects them to your own life through speeches, landmarks and a letter to your representatives.
1 k The Constitution :: What the Constitution is and what it does.
2 k The Preamble :: The six purposes stated in the Preamble and how they affect your family and community.
3 k The three branches :: The functions of the three branches of government and how they balance each other.
3a k What each branch does :: The function of each branch of the federal government.
3b k Separation of powers :: Why powers are divided among the branches.
3c k Checks and balances :: How each branch checks the others.
3d k Getting involved :: How citizens can take part in each branch.
4 k Founding documents :: Important national documents and ideals, such as the Declaration of Independence, the Bill of Rights and the national motto.
4a k The Declaration of Independence :: The purpose and meaning of the Declaration of Independence.
4b k The Bill of Rights :: The Bill of Rights and the Fourteenth Amendment.
4c k The national motto :: What “E Pluribus Unum” means.
5 d Following national issues :: Following the news for several days, discussing national issues and how one affects you, your family and community.
6 d A historic speech :: Studying an approved historic speech: its author, context, content and importance.
7 a National landmarks :: Two of four options, such as visiting a historic landmark, touring a capitol or federal facility, or researching a national monument.
7a a Historic site :: Visiting a historic landmark or site on the National Register.
7b a Capitol building :: Touring your state or federal capitol.
7c a Federal facility :: Touring a federal facility.
7d a National monument :: Researching a national monument.
8 a Writing to Congress :: Identifying your members of Congress and writing to one about a national issue.

@owner mb.citizenship-in-the-world
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/skills/merit-badges/digital-resource-guides/citizenship-in-the-world/contents/ | Citizenship in the World Digital Resource Guide (scouting.org)
@source https://www.scouting.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Major-Requirement-Changes-as-of-1_1_2026.pdf | Major Requirement Changes as of January 1, 2026 (scouting.org)
@note Requirements 6 and 7 changed for 2026; requirement 6 became three required parts.
@desc Citizenship in the World explores what it means to be a citizen of a connected world: how people become U.S. citizens, how nations pursue their interests, international law and trade, kinds of government, diplomacy, and connecting with people from other countries.
1 d World citizenship :: What world citizenship means to you and what it takes to be a good world citizen.
2 k Becoming a citizen :: How people become U.S. citizens, the rights, duties and obligations of citizenship, and how they compare with other countries.
3 m National interest and economies :: A current world event and national interest, and how a country’s geography and resources shape its economy.
3a d National interest :: How national interest affects a country’s actions in a current world event.
3b k Geography and economy :: How a foreign country’s geography, natural resources and climate shape its economy and partnerships.
4 k International relations :: Options covering international and national law, a current trade issue, and international organizations.
5 k Kinds of government :: Options covering constitutional and non-constitutional governments and governments in power today.
6 k Diplomacy :: How governments are represented abroad, the roles of ambassadors and consuls, and passports and visas.
6a k Representation abroad :: How governments are represented in other countries.
6b k Ambassadors and consuls :: The roles of ambassadors, consuls and other diplomats.
6c k Passports and visas :: The purpose of passports and visas.
7 a Connecting with the world :: Options such as visiting the State Department’s website, meeting a Scout or student from another country, or taking part in an international event.

@owner mb.communication
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/skills/merit-badges/digital-resource-guides/communication/contents/ | Communication Digital Resource Guide (scouting.org)
@desc Communication builds the skills of listening, speaking, writing and presenting — from keeping a listening journal and giving a speech to reporting on a public meeting, teaching a lesson and running a program as master of ceremonies.
1 a Observing communication :: One option for studying how you and others communicate.
1a a Communication log :: Keeping a one-day log of the ways you communicate.
1b a Listening journal :: Keeping a journal of listening experiences over several days.
1c a Leading a discussion :: Leading a small-group discussion.
1d k Ways to communicate :: Listing ways to communicate and when each is ineffective.
2 a Presenting yourself or an idea :: A creative description of yourself, or a pitch for a product or idea.
3 a A speech :: Writing and giving a short speech to a group.
4 a Introducing a speaker :: Interviewing someone and preparing an introduction of them as a guest speaker.
5 a Reporting on a meeting :: Attending an approved public meeting with several viewpoints and writing an objective report.
6 a Teaching a skill :: Planning and giving a lesson that teaches or informs, then checking that it worked.
7 a Publishing :: One option: a letter to an editor, a web page or blog, or a printed newsletter, brochure or flyer.
8 l Master of ceremonies :: Planning an approved troop event such as a court of honor or campfire program, writing the script and serving as master of ceremonies.
9 a Communication careers :: Researching communication careers and the preparation one of them requires.

@owner mb.cooking
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/skills/merit-badges/digital-resource-guides/cooking/contents/ | Cooking Digital Resource Guide (scouting.org)
@source https://www.scouting.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Merit-Badge-Requirement-Updates-Effective-January-1-2025-Updated-12-12-24.pdf | Merit Badge Requirement Updates effective January 1, 2025 (scouting.org)
@desc Cooking covers food safety and nutrition, cooking methods, and planning, cooking and evaluating real meals at home, in camp and on the trail — with attention to allergies, budgets and cleanup.
@safety Food handling, stoves, fires and food allergies are safety-sensitive. Cook with appropriate supervision and follow food-safety guidance.
@study study.camp-ready
1 k! Health and safety :: Cooking hazards and first aid, safe storage and preparation of perishable food, food allergies and reading labels.
2 k Nutrition :: Food groups, limiting oils and sugars, tracking what you eat, reviewing your habits and understanding food labels.
3 k Cooking basics :: Cooking methods, stoves versus fires, timing a meal, and how taste, texture and smell affect eating.
4 a! Cooking at home :: Planning menus, making shopping lists, and preparing, timing and evaluating meals at home using several cooking methods.
5 a! Camp cooking :: Planning, cooking and evaluating meals and snacks in camp, with safe cleanup and Leave No Trace.
= mb.camping.8d, rank.first-class.2e
6 a! Trail and backpacking meals :: Planning lightweight meals, repackaging food, cooking on a hike and sharing gear.
7 a Careers or hobbies :: One option: exploring cooking careers, or how cooking skills support a hobby or healthy lifestyle.

@owner mb.personal-fitness
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/skills/merit-badges/digital-resource-guides/personal-fitness/contents/ | Personal Fitness Digital Resource Guide (scouting.org)
@source https://www.scouting.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Major-Requirement-Changes-as-of-1_1_2026.pdf | Major Requirement Changes as of January 1, 2026 (scouting.org)
@desc Personal Fitness looks at fitness in body, mind and spirit, then asks you to measure where you are, design a twelve-week exercise and nutrition program, follow it, and show what changed.
@safety Talk with a parent or guardian, and a doctor if needed, before starting a new exercise program. Substitutions are allowed when an exercise is not safe for you.
1 d Defining fitness :: Physical, mental, emotional, social and spiritual fitness, why they matter, and how they relate to the Scout Oath and Law.
2 d Monitoring your health :: Why regular physical and dental exams and daily oral care matter.
3 d Fitness habits :: Exercise, avoiding harmful substances, vaccinations, nutrition, healthy weight, sleep and time with family and friends.
4 a Measures of fitness :: Recording a timed mile, strength measures and a flexibility test.
5 a Pre-assessment :: Recording your test results, finding your strongest and weakest areas, logging food and drink, and setting diet goals.
6 a Planning a program :: A twelve-week plan with warm-up, cardio, strength, flexibility, cool-down and diet goals.
7 a Completing the program :: Following and logging the program, retesting along the way, showing improvement and discussing results.
8 a Careers or lifestyle :: One option: exploring fitness careers, or how a fitness activity supports a hobby or healthy lifestyle.

@owner mb.personal-management
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/3321625-Scouts-BSA-Requirements.pdf | 2025 Scouts BSA Requirements (scouting.org)
@desc Personal Management is practical money and time management: saving for a goal, running a thirteen-week budget, understanding investing, insurance and credit, managing your time for a week, planning a project, and exploring the cost of a career.
1 a A family savings goal :: Choosing a major family purchase, writing a savings plan and a shopping strategy.
2 a A thirteen-week budget :: Preparing a budget, comparing expected income and expenses, and tracking actual results.
3 d Money concepts :: Discussing several money concepts, such as the emotions around money, buyer’s remorse and charitable giving.
4 k Saving and investing :: Saving versus investing, return and risk, simple and compound interest, and diversification.
5 k Kinds of investments :: Common investments such as stocks, mutual funds, bonds, certificates of deposit and savings accounts.
6 k Insurance :: Common kinds of insurance and what they protect against.
7 k Loans and credit :: Loans and interest rates, ways to borrow, credit and debit cards, credit reports and reducing debt.
8 a Time management :: A to-do list, a seven-day schedule and diary, and reviewing what worked.
9 a A project plan :: A written plan for a project, with goal, timeline, resources and budget.
10 a Career costs :: Researching a career’s education and costs and how to cover them.
